mbeddings\n- BCS = max pairwise SCS within a batch\n- SDGD uses graph coloring on confusability graph (edge if SCS > threshold τ)\n- SDGD-zero (embedding only) vs SDGD-cal (empirical confusion matrix from 8 calibration examples)\n- SCS validated against empirical confusion: Spearman ρ=0.38\n\n### SDGD results:\n- MAVEN: single-pass F1=0.135 → SDGD-zero 0.175 → SDGD-cal 0.192 (per-type 0.195)\n- Few-NERD: single-pass 0.414 → SDGD-zero 0.437 → SDGD-cal 0.449 (per-type 0.470)\n- Random ablation confirms confusability-aware grouping adds beyond batch reduction\n- Cost-effectiveness: SDGD-cal at ~14% of per-type API cost\n\n## Your review should cover:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the contributions? Is the multi-channel model a genuine insight or obvious? Is SDGD a significant methodological advance?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines and prior work missing? Is the positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper influence future work? Is the problem important?\n4. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are claims properly scoped?\n5. **Comparison to existing approaches**: How does SDGD compare to LLM-PEE, UniNER, etc.?\n\nKey questions to consider:\n- Is the \"multi-channel\" framing genuinely novel, or is it a restatement of known phenomena (more types = harder, similar types = confusing)?\n- The SCS metric is just cosine similarity of type name embeddings — is this too simplistic? What about type definitions/descriptions?\n- How does SDGD compare to LLM-PEE's embedding-based partitioning? The paper claims LLM-PEE doesn't validate its approach, but does SDGD offer a fundamentally different mechanism?\n- The paper has citation rendering issues (\"?\") — how much does this affect evaluation?\n- Absolute F1 values are very low (0.135-0.449) — does this limit practical significance?\n\nProvide:\n- A structured review with sections for Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Minor Issues\n- A preliminary score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- Be rigorous but fair. Note both what the paper does well and where it falls short.\n\nReport in under 1500 words."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015FnrvQskGxtnXQwLSDytkq","session_id":"5eba5149-decb-42fd-9375-14d22c483592","uuid":"11a1ce4a-31e7-4bb4-a42c-cf3f36a39048","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:19:56.099Z"}
