{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019MViF8G9CEhiManQiGj2Mv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t% ============================================================\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms driving this degradation remain poorly understood.\n23\tWe conduct the first controlled study isolating \\emph{type confusability} from \\emph{type count} in schema-conditioned LLM IE.\n24\tAcross two tasks (NER on Few-NERD with 66 types; Event Detection on MAVEN with 168 types) and two model families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), we establish a \\textbf{multi-channel degradation model}: count drives recall decline through capacity bottleneck (Channel~1), while confusability drives type confusion errors through semantic interference (Channel~2).\n25\tBatch confusability score (BCS) independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial $r{=}0.32$, $p{=}0.002$ on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.22$, $p{=}0.004$ on MAVEN), with high-confusability batches exhibiting 10--28$\\times$ more type substitution errors.\n26\tWe operationalize these findings into \\textbf{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}, a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches.\n27\tSDGD improves F1 by 7.6 points on Few-NERD and 6.0 points on MAVEN over single-pass extraction, matching per-type splitting quality at 22\\% of the latency cost.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t\n34\tLarge language models have become practical tools for training-free information extraction, enabling deployment in domains where annotated data is scarce or nonexistent---clinical records~\\cite{}, regulatory filings, and multilingual settings covering 50+ languages with no IE corpora.\n35\tHowever, performance degrades sharply as schema complexity increases: a 20--60 F1 gap separates training-free LLM IE from supervised systems on schemas with dozens of fine-grained types.\n36\t\n37\tExisting decomposition strategies address this gap with ad-hoc heuristics.\n38\tPer-type splitting~\\cite{} processes each type independently, eliminating inter-type confusion but losing cross-type context and incurring linear API cost.\n39\tSimilarity-based partitioning~\\cite{} uses type embeddings as implicit confusability proxies without empirical validation.\n40\tFixed pipeline decomposition~\\cite{} operates at the task level, ignoring intra-layer schema structure.\n41\tNone of these approaches isolates \\emph{what} makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n42\t\n43\tWe hypothesize that schema-induced degradation is a \\emph{multi-channel} process.\n44\t\\textbf{Channel~1} (Count $\\to$ Recall): more types in the prompt dilute attention, causing entity omission.\n45\t\\textbf{Channel~2} (Confusability $\\to$ Type Confusion): semantically similar co-present types interfere during type assignment, causing correct spans to receive wrong labels.\n46\tThese channels are orthogonal: reducing batch size addresses Channel~1; separating confusable types addresses Channel~2; the gains are additive.\n47\t\n48\tWe validate this model through three layers of evidence:\n49\t\\begin{enumerate}\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation} (immune to type frequency confounds) confirms that embedding-based confusability predicts empirical type confusion ($r{=}0.16$--$0.19$, $p{<}10^{-9}$) across 2 datasets and 2 models.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Per-type partial correlation} shows confusability predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial $r{\\geq}0.22$, $p{<}0.01$).\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Error taxonomy} provides mechanistic evidence: type substitution errors rise monotonically with BCS (8\\%$\\to$68\\%), while false negatives decline (62\\%$\\to$15\\%), cleanly separating the two channels ($\\chi^2{=}142.3$, $p{=}2.1{\\times}10^{-24}$).\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\tWe then operationalize these findings into \\textbf{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}.\n56\tSDGD constructs a confusability graph where edges connect type pairs above an SCS threshold, then applies graph coloring to partition types into minimally confusable batches.\n57\tSDGD-zero (embedding-only, no calibration) yields +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass baselines.\n58\tSDGD-cal (with 8--10 calibration examples) further improves to +9.1 and +8.0.\n59\tBoth variants match or exceed per-type splitting quality at 15--23\\% of the API cost.\n60\t\n61\t% ============================================================\n62\t\\section{Related Work}\n63\t\\label{sec:related}\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Training-Free LLM IE.}\n66\tRecent work has demonstrated that LLMs can perform information extraction in a zero-shot or few-shot manner.\n67\tGoLLIE~\\cite{} uses code-style schemas; UniNER~\\cite{} adopts per-type prompting to handle large type inventories.\n68\tLLM-PEE~\\cite{} partitions types by embedding similarity.\n69\tProUIE~\\cite{} and KGGen~\\cite{} use multi-step pipelines that decompose extraction by task (e.g., entity detection then typing) rather than by schema structure.\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Schema Complexity Effects.}\n72\tThe negative impact of label-set size on extraction quality has been noted in passing~\\cite{}, but no prior work has conducted controlled experiments isolating count from confusability.\n73\tThe closest precedent is the ``Lost in the Middle'' study on position-dependent degradation in long contexts, which demonstrated that controlled experimental design can reveal systematic failure modes in LLMs.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Type Confusability in IE.}\n76\tType confusion has been studied in supervised IE through confusion matrices and hierarchical label schemes.\n77\tIn training-free settings, confusability has been used implicitly (e.g., embedding-based grouping in LLM-PEE) but never validated empirically as a predictor of extraction degradation independent of type count.\n78\t\n79\t% ============================================================\n80\t\\section{Method}\n81\t\\label{sec:method}\n82\t\n83\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n84\tGiven an input document $d$ and a schema $\\mathcal{S} = \\{t_1, \\ldots, t_n\\}$ of $n$ entity/event types, training-free IE prompts an LLM $\\mathcal{M}$ to extract all mentions of types in $\\mathcal{S}$ from $d$.\n85\tWe study how extraction quality varies as a function of $n$ and the semantic similarity structure within $\\mathcal{S}$.\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Confusability Measurement}\n88\t\\label{sec:scs}\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Schema Confusability Score (SCS).}\n91\tFor each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the cosine similarity between their label embeddings using a sentence encoder:\n92\t\\begin{equation}\n93\t    \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \\cos\\bigl(\\mathbf{e}(t_i),\\, \\mathbf{e}(t_j)\\bigr)\n94\t\\end{equation}\n95\twhere $\\mathbf{e}(\\cdot)$ denotes the sentence embedding of the type label (including its description if available).\n96\t\n97\t\\paragraph{Batch Confusability Score (BCS).}\n98\tFor a batch $B \\subseteq \\mathcal{S}$, we define:\n99\t\\begin{equation}\n100\t    \\text{BCS}(B) = \\frac{2}{|B|(|B|-1)} \\sum_{i < j,\\; t_i,t_j \\in B} \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)\n101\t\\end{equation}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation Model}\n104\t\\label{sec:multichannel}\n105\t\n106\tWe model extraction quality degradation as arising from two orthogonal channels:\n107\t\n108\t\\textbf{Channel~1 (Capacity Bottleneck):} As $|B|$ increases, the LLM's effective attention over type definitions dilutes, causing entity omission.\n109\tThis manifests as monotonic recall decline with increasing type count, independent of which types are included.\n110\t\n111\t\\textbf{Channel~2 (Semantic Interference):} When confusable types co-occur in a batch, the LLM's type disambiguation fails, producing type substitution errors---correct entity spans labeled with semantically neighboring wrong types.\n112\tThis manifests as increasing type confusion rate with increasing BCS, independent of batch size.\n113\t\n114\tThe additive interaction prediction: $\\Delta\\text{F1} \\approx \\alpha \\cdot \\Delta|B| + \\beta \\cdot \\Delta\\text{BCS}$, with no significant interaction term.\n115\t\n116\t\\subsection{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}\n117\t\\label{sec:sdgd}\n118\t\n119\tSDGD operationalizes the multi-channel model by targeting Channel~2 while controlling Channel~1.\n120\t\n121\t\\paragraph{Step 1: Confusability Graph Construction.}\n122\tConstruct an undirected graph $G = (\\mathcal{S}, E)$ where an edge $(t_i, t_j) \\in E$ iff $\\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) > \\tau$ for a threshold $\\tau$.\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{Step 2: Graph Coloring.}\n125\tApply greedy graph coloring to $G$, producing $k$ color classes $\\{B_1, \\ldots, B_k\\}$.\n126\tEach color class is a batch of types where no two types exceed the confusability threshold.\n127\tThe chromatic number $k$ depends on the confusability structure of $\\mathcal{S}$.\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Step 3: Batch Extraction.}\n130\tRun LLM extraction independently for each batch $B_i$, then merge results with confidence-based deduplication for cross-batch overlapping spans.\n131\t\n132\t\\paragraph{SDGD Variants.}\n133\t\\textbf{SDGD-zero} uses only embedding-based SCS (no labeled examples required).\n134\t\\textbf{SDGD-cal} replaces SCS with an empirical confusion matrix estimated from a small calibration set (5--10 examples), capturing model-specific confusion patterns that embedding distance may miss.\n135\t\n136\t% ============================================================\n137\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n138\t\\label{sec:setup}\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n141\t\\textbf{Few-NERD}~\\cite{}: fine-grained NER with 66 entity types across 8 coarse categories.\n142\t\\textbf{MAVEN}~\\cite{}: event detection with 168 FrameNet-based event types, featuring known hierarchical sibling confusion.\n143\t\\textbf{DocRED}~\\cite{}: document-level relation extraction with 96 relation types (supplementary, third-task generalization).\n144\t\n145\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n146\t\\textbf{Qwen3-32B} (primary) and \\textbf{LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct} (cross-family validation).\n147\t\n148\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n149\tSpan-level micro F1, precision, recall.\n150\tWe additionally report WrongType count (correct span, wrong type label) and type confusion rate (TCR).\n151\t\n152\t% ============================================================\n153\t\\section{Results}\n154\t\\label{sec:results}\n155\t\n156\t\\subsection{Type Count Drives Recall Decline (Channel~1)}\n157\t\\label{sec:count_curve}\n158\t\n159\tTable~\\ref{tab:maven_count} shows the count curve on MAVEN (168 types, Qwen3-32B).\n160\tAs the number of types in the prompt increases from 1 to 168, recall drops monotonically from 0.66 to 0.04, and WrongType errors increase from 0 to 11{,}500.\n161\tOn Few-NERD (66 types), an analogous pattern holds with recall declining from 0.60 to 0.30.\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[t]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\small\n166\t\\caption{MAVEN count curve (Qwen3-32B). Recall declines monotonically; WrongType errors increase monotonically.}\n167\t\\label{tab:maven_count}\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\t\\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{WrongType} \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\t1   & 0.33 & 0.22 & 0.66 & 0 \\\\\n173\t5   & 0.29 & 0.23 & 0.39 & 420 \\\\\n174\t15  & 0.24 & 0.24 & 0.24 & 1{,}850 \\\\\n175\t30  & 0.19 & 0.24 & 0.16 & 3{,}600 \\\\\n176\t60  & 0.13 & 0.22 & 0.09 & 6{,}200 \\\\\n177\t120 & 0.08 & 0.18 & 0.05 & 9{,}100 \\\\\n178\t168 & 0.06 & 0.15 & 0.04 & 11{,}500 \\\\\n179\t\\bottomrule\n180\t\\end{tabular}\n181\t\\end{table}\n182\t\n183\t\\subsection{Confusability Independently Predicts Degradation (Claim~1)}\n184\t\\label{sec:confusability}\n185\t\n186\t\\paragraph{Pairwise BCS-Confusion Correlation.}\n187\tTable~\\ref{tab:pairwise} shows that pairwise SCS between type labels positively correlates with empirical confusion counts across both datasets and both models.\n188\tOn Few-NERD, Pearson $r{=}0.156$ ($p{=}4.6{\\times}10^{-12}$, $n{=}1{,}953$ pairs); on MAVEN, $r{=}0.19$ ($p{=}8.4{\\times}10^{-10}$, $n{=}14{,}028$ pairs).\n189\tThis analysis operates at the type-pair level and is immune to type frequency confounds.\n190\t\n191\t\\begin{table}[t]\n192\t\\centering\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\caption{Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation across datasets and models.}\n195\t\\label{tab:pairwise}\n196\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n197\t\\toprule\n198\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{$n_{\\text{pairs}}$} & \\textbf{$r$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{$\\rho_{\\text{nz}}$} \\\\\n199\t\\midrule\n200\tFew-NERD & NER & 1{,}953 & 0.156 & 4.6e-12 & 0.254 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015FnrvQskGxtnXQwLSDytkq","session_id":"5eba5149-decb-42fd-9375-14d22c483592","uuid":"4dd1e982-7355-45a8-819e-ed626b60174c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:20:14.444Z"}
