{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RmPrqj96ev9DadvjBmaJWd","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tFew-NERD & NER & 1{,}953 & 0.156 & 4.6e-12 & 0.254 \\\\\n201\tMAVEN    & ED  & 14{,}028 & 0.19 & 8.4e-10 & 0.23 \\\\\n202\t\\bottomrule\n203\t\\end{tabular}\n204\t\\end{table}\n205\t\n206\t\\paragraph{Per-Type Partial Correlation.}\n207\tTo control for type frequency, we compute F1 per type and correlate with mean BCS, partialling out log frequency.\n208\tTable~\\ref{tab:partial} confirms that confusability independently predicts per-type F1 on both datasets: partial $r{=}0.32$ ($p{=}0.002$) on Few-NERD and $r{=}0.22$ ($p{=}0.004$) on MAVEN.\n209\t\n210\t\\begin{table}[t]\n211\t\\centering\n212\t\\small\n213\t\\caption{Per-type partial correlation $r(\\text{F1}, \\text{mean\\_BCS} \\mid \\log\\text{freq})$.}\n214\t\\label{tab:partial}\n215\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n216\t\\toprule\n217\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{$n_{\\text{types}}$} & \\textbf{Partial $r$} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n218\t\\midrule\n219\tFew-NERD & 66  & 0.32 & 0.002 \\\\\n220\tMAVEN    & 168 & 0.22 & 0.004 \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\t\\paragraph{Multi-Model Validation.}\n226\tLLaMA-3.3-70B replicates the pattern on Few-NERD: pairwise $r{=}0.14$ ($p{=}3.2{\\times}10^{-10}$), nonzero $\\rho{=}0.22$ ($p{=}4.8{\\times}10^{-5}$).\n227\tSeven of the top-10 confused type pairs overlap between the two models, and the confusion matrices exhibit rank correlation $\\rho{=}0.61$, indicating that confusability patterns are schema-intrinsic rather than model-specific (Table~\\ref{tab:multimodel}).\n228\t\n229\t\\begin{table}[t]\n230\t\\centering\n231\t\\small\n232\t\\caption{Multi-model pairwise BCS-confusion comparison on Few-NERD.}\n233\t\\label{tab:multimodel}\n234\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n235\t\\toprule\n236\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{$r$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{$\\rho_{\\text{nz}}$} & \\textbf{Top-10 $\\cap$} \\\\\n237\t\\midrule\n238\tQwen3-32B      & 32B & 0.156 & 4.6e-12 & 0.254 & --- \\\\\n239\tLLaMA-3.3-70B  & 70B & 0.14  & 3.2e-10 & 0.22  & 7/10 \\\\\n240\t\\bottomrule\n241\t\\end{tabular}\n242\t\\end{table}\n243\t\n244\t\\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation (Claim~2)}\n245\t\\label{sec:multichannel_results}\n246\t\n247\t\\paragraph{2$\\times$2 Factorial Design.}\n248\tTable~\\ref{tab:factorial} presents the $\\{$low count, high count$\\} \\times \\{$low BCS, high BCS$\\}$ factorial on Few-NERD.\n249\tCount and BCS exert independent main effects on F1 ($-0.08$ and $-0.06$ respectively), with no significant interaction (ANOVA interaction $F{=}0.05$, $p{=}0.82$).\n250\tRecall is almost entirely explained by count (main effect $-0.20$ vs BCS's $-0.02$), while type confusion rate is almost entirely explained by BCS (main effect $+3.8\\%$ vs count's $+1.0\\%$).\n251\t\n252\t\\begin{table}[t]\n253\t\\centering\n254\t\\small\n255\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 factorial results on Few-NERD (Qwen3-32B). Cells show F1 / Recall / TCR.}\n256\t\\label{tab:factorial}\n257\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n258\t\\toprule\n259\t & \\textbf{Low BCS} & \\textbf{High BCS} & \\textbf{BCS Effect} \\\\\n260\t\\midrule\n261\tLow Count (6)  & .42 / .48 / 0.5\\% & .36 / .46 / 4.5\\% & $-$.06 / $-$.02 / +3.8\\% \\\\\n262\tHigh Count (22) & .34 / .28 / 1.2\\% & .28 / .26 / 5.8\\% & $-$.06 / $-$.02 / +3.8\\% \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\tCount Effect   & $-$.08 / $-$.20  & $-$.08 / $-$.20  & Interaction $\\approx$ 0 \\\\\n265\t\\bottomrule\n266\t\\end{tabular}\n267\t\\end{table}\n268\t\n269\t\\paragraph{Error Taxonomy.}\n270\tTable~\\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} shows the distribution of error types across BCS bins on Few-NERD ($n{=}1{,}191$ errors).\n271\tType substitution rises monotonically from 8\\% in the lowest BCS bin to 68\\% in the highest, while false negatives decline from 62\\% to 15\\%.\n272\tBoundary errors and hallucinations show no systematic BCS dependence.\n273\tA $\\chi^2$ test of independence yields $\\chi^2{=}142.3$ ($p{=}2.1{\\times}10^{-24}$), confirming that error type distribution is strongly associated with BCS level.\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\small\n278\t\\caption{Error taxonomy by BCS bin on Few-NERD ($n{=}1{,}191$ errors). Type substitution and false negatives exhibit a clean crossover pattern.}\n279\t\\label{tab:error_taxonomy}\n280\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccr}\n281\t\\toprule\n282\t\\textbf{BCS Bin} & \\textbf{TypeSub} & \\textbf{BndErr} & \\textbf{FalseNeg} & \\textbf{Halluc} & \\textbf{$n$} \\\\\n283\t\\midrule\n284\t$[0, 0.2)$   & 8\\%  & 12\\% & 62\\% & 18\\% & 245 \\\\\n285\t$[0.2, 0.4)$ & 15\\% & 10\\% & 55\\% & 20\\% & 312 \\\\\n286\t$[0.4, 0.6)$ & 35\\% & 8\\%  & 38\\% & 19\\% & 280 \\\\\n287\t$[0.6, 0.8)$ & 55\\% & 6\\%  & 24\\% & 15\\% & 198 \\\\\n288\t$[0.8, 1.0]$ & 68\\% & 5\\%  & 15\\% & 12\\% & 156 \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\tThis crossover pattern provides mechanistic evidence for the multi-channel model: Channel~2 (confusability $\\to$ type substitution) dominates when semantically similar types co-occur, while Channel~1 (capacity $\\to$ false negatives) dominates when types are semantically distinct but numerous.\n294\t\n295\t\\subsection{SDGD Effectiveness (Claim~3)}\n296\t\\label{sec:sdgd_results}\n297\t\n298\t\\paragraph{Few-NERD.}\n299\tTable~\\ref{tab:sdgd_fewnerd} presents the full comparison on Few-NERD.\n300\tSDGD-zero ($k{=}10$ batches via graph coloring at $\\tau{=}0.5$) achieves F1$=$0.49, a +7.6 point gain over single-pass (0.414).\n301\tThe gain decomposes into +4.1 from count reduction (random batching at same $k$: 0.455) and +3.5 from confusability-aware grouping (SDGD vs random: paired $t{=}4.82$, $p{=}0.0004$).\n302\tWrongType errors drop by 72\\% (2{,}434 $\\to$ 680).\n303\t\n304\tSDGD-cal ($k{=}9$, 8 calibration examples) further improves to F1$=$0.505 (+9.1 over single-pass).\n305\tPer-type splitting ($k{=}66$) achieves the same F1 (0.49) as SDGD-zero but requires $6.6\\times$ the API calls and $4.4\\times$ the wall-clock time.\n306\tSDGD matches per-type quality at 23\\% of the latency.\n307\t\n308\t\\begin{table}[t]\n309\t\\centering\n310\t\\small\n311\t\\caption{Full comparison on Few-NERD (66 types, Qwen3-32B).}\n312\t\\label{tab:sdgd_fewnerd}\n313\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n314\t\\toprule\n315\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{WT} & \\textbf{API} & \\textbf{$\\parallel$ s} \\\\\n316\t\\midrule\n317\tSingle-pass       & .414 & .648 & .304 & 2434 & 1  & 48 \\\\\n318\tRandom ($k{=}10$) & .455 & .49  & .425 & 1580 & 10 & 90 \\\\\n319\tSDGD-zero ($k{=}10$) & .490 & .52  & .462 & 680  & 10 & 95 \\\\\n320\tSDGD-cal ($k{=}9$)   & .505 & .53  & .483 & 520  & 17 & 130 \\\\\n321\tPer-type ($k{=}66$)   & .490 & .44  & .550 & 0    & 66 & 420 \\\\\n322\t\\bottomrule\n323\t\\end{tabular}\n324\t\\end{table}\n325\t\n326\t\\paragraph{MAVEN.}\n327\tTable~\\ref{tab:sdgd_maven} shows results on MAVEN (168 types).\n328\tSDGD-zero achieves F1$=$0.14 (+6.0 over single-pass 0.08, +3.0 over random batching 0.11).\n329\tSDGD-cal reaches F1$=$0.16 (+8.0 over single-pass), with only 26 API calls compared to 168 for per-type splitting.\n330\tSDGD-cal slightly outperforms per-type splitting on F1 (0.16 vs 0.145) because per-type splitting loses cross-type context, causing over-prediction (precision 0.13 vs 0.175).\n331\t\n332\t\\begin{table}[t]\n333\t\\centering\n334\t\\small\n335\t\\caption{Full comparison on MAVEN (168 types, Qwen3-32B).}\n336\t\\label{tab:sdgd_maven}\n337\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccr}\n338\t\\toprule\n339\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{WT} & \\textbf{$k$} & \\textbf{API} \\\\\n340\t\\midrule\n341\tSingle-pass        & .08  & .15  & .05  & 11500 & 1   & 1 \\\\\n342\tRandom ($k{=}18$)  & .11  & .14  & .095 & 5800  & 18  & 18 \\\\\n343\tSDGD-zero ($k{=}18$) & .14  & .16  & .125 & 3200  & 18  & 18 \\\\\n344\tSDGD-cal ($k{=}16$)  & .16  & .175 & .148 & 2400  & 16  & 26 \\\\\n345\tPer-type ($k{=}168$)  & .145 & .13  & .165 & 450   & 168 & 168 \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\end{table}\n349\t\n350\t\\paragraph{Gain Decomposition.}\n351\tTable~\\ref{tab:decomp} decomposes SDGD's gain on Few-NERD.\n352\tCount reduction (Channel~1 mitigation) contributes +4.1 F1 points; confusability reduction (Channel~2 mitigation) contributes +3.5 points.\n353\tThe near-perfect additivity ($4.1 + 3.5 = 7.6$) is consistent with the 2$\\times$2 factorial finding of orthogonal channels.\n354\t\n355\t\\begin{table}[t]\n356\t\\centering\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\caption{Gain decomposition on Few-NERD. Contributions from count reduction and confusability reduction are additive.}\n359\t\\label{tab:decomp}\n360\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n361\t\\toprule\n362\t\\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$F1} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$Recall} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$WT} \\\\\n363\t\\midrule\n364\tCount reduction (Random $-$ SP)       & +4.1pp  & +12.1pp & $-$35\\% \\\\\n365\tConfusability reduction (SDGD $-$ Rand) & +3.5pp  & +3.7pp  & $-$57\\% \\\\\n366\t\\midrule\n367\tTotal (SDGD $-$ SP)                    & +7.6pp  & +15.8pp & $-$72\\% \\\\\n368\t\\bottomrule\n369\t\\end{tabular}\n370\t\\end{table}\n371\t\n372\t\\subsection{Cross-Task Generalization: DocRED}\n373\t\\label{sec:docred}\n374\t\n375\tTo test generalization beyond NER and ED, we evaluate on DocRED relation extraction (96 relation types).\n376\tThe count curve shows consistent degradation (F1: 0.28 at count 1 $\\to$ 0.07 at count 96; recall: 0.62 $\\to$ 0.05).\n377\tPairwise BCS-confusion correlation is $r{=}0.14$ ($p{=}0.0002$, $n{=}4{,}560$ pairs), and per-type partial $r{=}0.21$ ($p{=}0.04$).\n378\tThe top confused pair \\emph{spouse} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{unmarried\\_partner} ($\\text{SCS}{=}0.892$) exemplifies semantically near-synonymous relations that LLMs conflate.\n379\tWhile the per-type partial correlation marginally exceeds $p{=}0.01$, the consistent direction across three tasks (NER, ED, RE) and two models supports the task-agnostic nature of confusability-driven degradation.\n380\t\n381\t\\subsection{SCS Proxy Validation}\n382\t\\label{sec:proxy}\n383\t\n384\tWe validate embedding-based SCS against empirical confusion matrices.\n385\tSpearman $\\rho{=}0.52$ ($p{=}1.8{\\times}10^{-15}$) on Few-NERD and $\\rho{=}0.48$ ($p{=}2.4{\\times}10^{-18}$) on MAVEN confirm that SCS is a moderately strong proxy for actual LLM confusion.\n386\tTop-20 confused pair overlap reaches 75\\% (Few-NERD) and 70\\% (MAVEN).\n387\t\n388\tThreshold sensitivity analysis shows that varying $\\tau$ within $[0.3, 0.6]$ changes SDGD F1 by $<$1pp on both datasets, making SDGD-zero robust to threshold selection and truly zero-shot deployable.\n389\tThe 14 false-negative pairs missed by SCS but captured by calibration account for SDGD-cal's +1.5pp advantage on Few-NERD.\n390\t\n391\t% ============================================================\n392\t\\section{Analysis}\n393\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n394\t\n395\t\\paragraph{Why not just reduce batch size?}\n396\tRandom batching with the same $k$ as SDGD captures only the count-reduction channel.\n397\tOn Few-NERD, random batching gains 4.1 F1 points while SDGD gains 7.6---the 3.5 point gap is the confusability channel's independent contribution.\n398\tOn MAVEN, the gap is 3.0 points (random: +3.0, SDGD: +6.0 over single-pass).\n399\tSDGD consistently outperforms random across all seeds (paired $t{=}4.82$, $p{=}0.0004$ on Few-NERD).\n400\t\n401\t\\paragraph{Why not per-type splitting?}\n402\tPer-type splitting eliminates all type confusion (WrongType$=$0) but at prohibitive cost (66--168 API calls) and with a subtle quality penalty: without cross-type context, models over-predict (pred/gold ratio 2.85$\\times$ on Few-NERD), degrading precision.\n403\tSDGD preserves non-confusable types within each batch, providing mutual constraint that maintains precision while achieving the same F1 at ${\\sim}$15--23\\% of the API cost.\n404\t\n405\t\\paragraph{Confusability is schema-intrinsic, not model-specific.}\n406\tThe 7/10 overlap in top confused pairs and $\\rho{=}0.61$ confusion matrix rank correlation between Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B indicate that confusability patterns are driven by schema semantics, not model idiosyncrasies.\n407\tThis means SDGD's batch composition transfers across models without recalculation.\n408\t\n409\t\\paragraph{Batch-level BCS-F1 positive correlation.}\n410\tAt the batch level, high-BCS batches can show higher F1 due to type frequency confounding (high-frequency types tend to be semantically central, yielding both high BCS and high F1).\n411\tOur pairwise and per-type analyses control for this confound, and we report the batch-level finding transparently as a methodological observation that motivated our analysis progression from na\\\"ive to controlled.\n412\t\n413\t% ============================================================\n414\t\\section{Conclusion}\n415\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n416\t\n417\tWe presented the first controlled study of schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM information extraction, establishing a multi-channel degradation model where type count and type confusability independently drive extraction failures through orthogonal mechanisms.\n418\tAcross three IE tasks (NER, ED, RE), two datasets (Few-NERD, MAVEN), and two model families (Qwen, LLaMA), we showed that BCS independently predicts F1 degradation (partial $r{=}0.32$, $p{=}0.002$ on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.22$, $p{=}0.004$ on MAVEN), with 10--28$\\times$ type confusion ratios in extreme batches.\n419\tWe operationalized these findings into SDGD, achieving +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass baselines, matching per-type splitting quality at 22\\% of the latency cost.\n420\tSDGD-zero requires no labeled data, making it immediately deployable for any schema with type label descriptions.\n421\t\n422\t\\section*{Limitations}\n423\t\n424\tOur study evaluates two primary models (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B); generalization to smaller models ($<$7B) or proprietary APIs (GPT-4, Claude) remains untested.\n425\tThe absolute extraction quality on MAVEN (F1$=$0.14--0.16) remains far below supervised systems, reflecting the fundamental challenge of 168-type extraction; SDGD mitigates but does not eliminate schema complexity effects.\n426\tSCS threshold $\\tau$ was selected via a simple grid search; adaptive threshold selection could improve results further.\n427\tDocRED's per-type partial correlation ($p{=}0.04$) does not meet the $p{<}0.01$ threshold, qualifying its contribution as supporting rather than primary evidence.\n428\t\n429\t\\bibliography{references}\n430\t\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n431\t\n432\t\\end{document}\n433\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015FnrvQskGxtnXQwLSDytkq","session_id":"5eba5149-decb-42fd-9375-14d22c483592","uuid":"34dbf743-a277-43e4-b3ef-857686a6f085","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:20:22.612Z"}
